.TH KEYHOLD 2
.SH NAME
keyhold: mk, key, edge \- which keys are held down now
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "keyboard.m";
include "keyhold.m";
keyhold := load Keyhold Keyhold->PATH;
Binding: import keyhold;

Binding: adt {
	code: array of int;
	bit:  array of int;

	key:  fn(h: self ref Binding, held, k: int): int;
};

mk:   fn(binds: array of (int, int)): ref Binding;
edge: fn(held, prev: int): int;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Keyhold
turns the stream of key events a window delivers into the set of actions
held down right now.
That is a small thing, and it exists because of two facts about Inferno
rather than anything about the program using it.
.PP
The first is that a window gets key
.I releases
only if it asked for them.
.IR Wmclient (2)'s
.I startinput
must be given
.B keyup
as well as
.BR kbd ,
and
.IR wm (1)
drops releases for a client that did not \- see
.B Keyupstarted
in
.BR appl/wm/wm.b .
A program that forgets sees only presses, and every key it reads sticks
down for good.
.PP
The second is how a release arrives.
.B include/keyboard.h
encodes it as
.BR "Keyup | (key & 0x7ff)" ,
where
.B Keyup
is
.BR Spec|0x800 .
So a release carries only the low eleven bits of the key, and \- because
every arrow and function key already carries
.B Spec
\- a naive
.B "k & Keyup"
says a plain Left arrow is a release.
The whole of
.B Spec
has to match, which is why
.I wm
has an
.I iskeyup
rather than a mask.
.PP
.I Mk
takes a table of (key, bit) pairs.
A bit may appear more than once, which is how a program offers both the
arrow keys and
.B wasd
for the same action.
.I Binding.key
folds one event into a held set and returns the new one; a key the table
does not mention changes nothing rather than clearing the set.
.I Edge
returns what is held now and was not held then, for the actions that
should happen once a press rather than every frame the key is down.
.PP
Nothing here names the buttons.
What a button means is the caller's business: the same bit is jump in one
program and fire in another, and a table the program writes is a table
the program can read \- which matters, because a binding taken twice is
a binding taken once.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/keyhold.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR keyboard (2),
.IR wmclient (2),
.IR keyholdtest (1),
.IR tiles (2)
.SH BUGS
It knows nothing about shift, control or the other held modifiers, which
arrive as ordinary keys with their own codes and can simply be bound.
.PP
The table is searched linearly for every event.
Events arrive at the speed a person types, so this has never mattered.
